8.What is a neuron in ANN? Explain its working.
The smallest unit of an Artifical Neural Network, inspired by biological brain neurons.
Each neuron takes inputs, multiplies them with weights, adds a bias, applies an activation functions and
produces an output.

9.What are weights in neural networks? Why are they important?
Numerical values representing the strength of connections between neurons.
High weight - strong influence of input on output.
Low weight - weak/no influence.

They are learned during training and directly affect model accuracy.
Without proper weights, the network cannot capture relationship in data.

10.What is bias in ANN? Why is bias required?
An additional parameters that allows shifting the activation functions
It helps the model fit data better, even if all inputs are zero.




















